#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""qb-reconcile — does the plan still describe the present?

`plan_read` computes one answer, `next`, and every agent that starts cold acts on
it. Nothing checked it against reality. On 2026-08-20 the plan's ranks 2 and 4
pointed at PRs #182 and #211, both merged ninety minutes earlier, and `next`
returned rank 2 — finished work, offered as the thing to do. `idle_days: 0.0,
stale: false` sat beside it, because staleness measures time-since-touched and
not agreement-with-reality: an item can be wrong and fresh at the same time.

This walks the plan's refs against GitHub and the board's own review record and
reports every disagreement it can establish. It is the deterministic half of
#232 in its cheapest possible form, and #232 needs it regardless — an orderer
cannot order a plan that does not describe the present.

WHAT IT IS NOT. No agent, no claims, no hook wiring. It resolves refs, compares,
prints, and exits; the writes it can make are one board post (`--post`) and, since
#552, one plan transition (`--apply`).

THE RULE THAT CHANGED, AND HOW FAR (#552). This paragraph used to read "it NEVER
edits the plan: 'this item looks done' is a candidate for a human or a `plan_done`
call, not a state transition to make behind their back". The detector was right and
unheard. Ranks 1-3 of this repo's own plan were closed work, flagged
`done_candidate` and re-confirmed every fifteen minutes for days; on lexray
thirteen finished items sat on top of a thirty-one-item ordered plan with every one
of their claims already expired. Picking work up writes an item (#427) and nothing
writes it back, and the two halves decay differently — a claim expires on its TTL
and needs nobody, an item has no TTL at all — so the residue is permanent and it
lands at the TOP, because picked-up items rank first.

So the rule is NARROWED, not dropped: it never **judges**. `--apply` completes an
item whose own ref is a pull request GitHub reports `MERGED`, and nothing else.
That the work merged is a fact about the code, and recording it is bookkeeping.
`dropped` stays a decision — the plan's own model says so — and a tool that
inferred it would erase the distinction the model exists to keep.

AND ONLY THAT LEG, DELIBERATELY. #552's predicate is three-legged: an item is done
when its PR merges, or its issue is closed, or the agent releases it. Only the
first is acted on here, and the `test`-branch flow is why. `MERGED` is
base-branch agnostic — GitHub says it whatever the base — so that leg reads the
same in a repo whose PRs land on `test` as in one where they land on the default
branch. The issue leg does not: a closing keyword only fires on a merge into the
DEFAULT branch, and an issue-ref item cannot see the pull request that implemented
it, because the item→PR edge is computed on every pass and stored nowhere (#396).
So an issue-ref `done_candidate` is declined by name, in the report, on every tick.
The residue stays visible rather than being cleared on a signal that does not mean
the same thing in every repo.

THE FIVE CONDITIONS (#255's table):

    done_candidate      item open, its work merged or closed-as-completed
    dropped_candidate   item open, its work closed unmerged / not-planned
    stale_claim         item claimed, but the claim does not describe the present
    note_contradicted   item's note asserts a readiness /review/findings denies
    untracked_pr        open PR no open plan item accounts for

REF KIND IS NOT ONE OF THEM. The first two conditions are "the item outlived its
work" and "the work was abandoned"; whether that work is spelled as a PR or an
issue is only how it is looked up. Nine of the fourteen items on this plan carry
`issue` refs, so a pass that read PR refs alone would be silent on two thirds of
it — while reporting, in the same breath, that it had checked the plan.

ABSENCE IS NOT INABILITY (#244, and the reason this file is shaped as it is).
Every condition has a third answer. A ref GitHub would not resolve, a claim
naming no session, a stop summary the board cannot attribute to one cycle — none
of those is "no disagreement", and each is reported in its own section with the
reason it could not be settled. The exit code says which happened: a run that
could not make a check does not get to look like a run that made it and found
nothing. This matters in practice today, not hypothetically — the deployed board
is v2.48 and `/review/findings` there returns no `cycles` field at all, so its
`stopped` cannot be attributed and this says so rather than guessing.

Exit codes:
    0   ran, every check completed (findings or not — a disagreement is the
        report, not an error)
    1   ran, but at least one check could not be made — or, under `--apply`, a
        write the pass decided on could not be made either
    2   could not run at all: no board, no `gh`, or bad arguments

Usage:
    qb-reconcile                     every repo the board's plan names
    qb-reconcile --repo owner/name   just that one
    qb-reconcile --json              the whole report as JSON (what #232 reads)
    qb-reconcile --post              post one `finding` — only when it CHANGED
    qb-reconcile --apply             complete items whose own PR is merged (#552)
    qb-reconcile --include-drafts    count draft PRs as untracked work too
    qb-reconcile --quiet             say nothing when there is nothing to say
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
from dataclasses import asdict, dataclass, field
from datetime import UTC, datetime, timedelta
from urllib.parse import urlencode

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
from qbdata import board_client, clip, short_repo  # noqa: E402

#: How many open PRs to ask each repo for. Higher than the dashboard's 30
#: because a truncated list here does not degrade a panel, it silently drops
#: `untracked_pr` findings — so the cap is generous AND reported when reached
#: (:func:`untracked_prs` raises it as an unknown rather than trimming quietly).
PR_FETCH_LIMIT = 100

#: Seconds for one `gh` call. Generous: a cron tick that takes a minute is fine,
#: one that hangs until the next tick is not.
GH_TIMEOUT = 45

#: How many plan items to ask for. The board's own cap is 200 and a plan is tens of
#: rows by design, so this is a backstop — but it is a cap all the same, and
#: `GET /plan` says whether it bit (`truncated`). That flag is READ rather than
#: assumed false: a reconcile pass that silently skipped the tail of the plan while
#: printing a report headed "every repo the board's plan names" would be committing
#: the exact error it exists to find.
#:
#: The plan is fetched with `include_done=true`, which is why this is 500 rather
#: than the board's own default of 200: the **repo set** this pass asks GitHub about
#: comes from every row the plan has, not only its open ones. A repo whose plan rows
#: are all done or dropped is still a repo the plan names, and it is the most likely
#: place for untracked work to be — dropping it from the scope means its open PRs
#: cannot be reported at all, in silence, under a report headed "every repo the
#: board's plan names". So history is in scope, history is what grows, and the cap
#: has to cover both while staying a cap (the board's own maximum is 1000).
PLAN_LIMIT = 500

#: The conditions a FINDING can carry, and the closed vocabulary `--json`
#: consumers group by. Deliberately not shared with the unknowns: a check that
#: could not be made is not always one condition's, and filing "the plan was
#: truncated" under `done_candidate` told a consumer grouping by condition that a
#: truncation was a done-candidate problem while the text underneath said it
#: invalidated `untracked_pr`.
CONDITIONS = (
    "done_candidate",
    "dropped_candidate",
    "stale_claim",
    "note_contradicted",
    "untracked_pr",
)

#: Conditions only an :class:`Unknown` carries — a check that is not any single
#: finding condition's. `ref_unresolved` is the ref lookup that answers conditions
#: 1 AND 2 at once; `plan_incomplete` is about the report, not about an item.
UNKNOWN_CONDITIONS = (
    "ref_unresolved",
    "plan_incomplete",
)

#: What a note has to say for :func:`note_asserts_ready` to read it as a claim
#: that a PR is ready to land. Deliberately a closed vocabulary rather than a
#: sentiment judgement: this half of `note_contradicted` is the half that CANNOT
#: be mechanical, so it is kept small, literal, and quoted back in the finding so
#: a reader can dismiss a bad match in one glance.
#:
#: `clean` carries a negative lookahead for "clean up" and its spellings. Without
#: it, a note saying "clean up the error handling" on a PR with open findings
#: reads as an assertion that the PR is clean — the one false positive this
#: vocabulary was always going to have, and the only one worth a special case.
#:
#: **Bare `green` is deliberately NOT here, and three live plan rows are why.**
#: It was, and on this repo's own plan it matched "this PR is what makes it
#: green", "should go green on its own" and "all checks were green at its last
#: push" — three for three, none of them an assertion that anything is ready. The
#: word belongs to CI, and CI is not the review record: a note about checks and a
#: denial from `/review/findings` are claims about different things, so pairing
#: them manufactures a contradiction out of two statements that never disagreed.
#: `panel is green` stays, because it names what it is talking about.
#:
#: **Every readiness word carries `\b` on both ends, and the reason is the same
#: three times over.** The negation guard below cannot catch a negator that is
#: INSIDE the word: "unclean after the rebase" matches a boundary-less `clean` at
#: offset 2 with `before == "un"`, no negator fires, and this reads a note saying
#: the reverse of the vocabulary as an assertion of readiness — the costliest false
#: positive available, because it manufactures a contradiction on the one condition
#: that cannot be checked mechanically. `unmergeable` was caught and fixed; `un`
#: clean and `un`landable shipped in the same tuple without it. The trailing
#: boundary earns its keep too: without it `clean` matched "cleaned up the tests"
#: and `review(?:ed)? clean` matched "reviewed cleanup pass".
_READY_PHRASES = (
    r"\bmergeable\b",
    r"\bclean(?!\s*[-\s]?ups?\b)(?!ing\b)\b",
    r"\blandable\b",
    r"ready to (?:land|merge|go)",
    r"ready for (?:merge|landing|land)",
    r"no (?:open |outstanding )?findings",
    r"nothing (?:left )?to fix",
    r"(?:all )?findings (?:all )?(?:fixed|resolved|addressed|cleared)",
    r"\breview(?:ed)? clean\b",
    r"panel (?:is )?(?:clean|done|green)\b",
)
_READY = re.compile("|".join(f"(?:{p})" for p in _READY_PHRASES), re.I)

#: Words that turn a readiness phrase into its opposite, looked for in the run of
#: text immediately before a match. "not ready to land" and "no longer clean" both
#: contain a phrase from the vocabulary and both say the reverse of it, so a
#: matcher without this reports the note as contradicted when the note and the
#: board agree — the false positive that costs the most, because it makes the pass
#: wrong about the one condition it cannot check mechanically.
_NEGATORS = re.compile(
    r"\b(?:not|never|no|nor|isn't|isnt|aren't|arent|wasn't|wasnt|won't|wont|can't|cant|"
    r"cannot|hasn't|hasnt|haven't|havent|don't|dont|doesn't|doesnt|without|far from|"
    r"nowhere near|less than|other than|anything but|nothing but)\b", re.I)

#: Where a negator's reach ENDS, looking backwards from a matched phrase. A
#: sentence boundary is the obvious one; a contrastive conjunction is the one that
#: matters, and it was found by this pass's own test suite. "not ready to land last
#: week, **but** the panel is clean now" makes a readiness claim in its second
#: clause, and a guard that let `not` cross the comma silenced it — a false
#: NEGATIVE, which is worse here than the false positive the guard was added for,
#: because a disagreement this pass fails to report is one nobody else is looking
#: for. So only the clause the phrase actually sits in is searched.
#: `but` is the load-bearing entry and the one with an exception: in "anything but
#: clean" it is part of the negation rather than a break in front of one, so those
#: two spellings do not end a negator's reach. Fixed-width lookbehinds, which is
#: all Python allows, and all this needs.
_CLAUSE_BREAK = re.compile(
    r"[.;!?]|(?<!anything )(?<!nothing )\b(?:but|however|although|though|whereas|yet)\b",
    re.I)

#: The negating prefix that survived the `\b` fix, because a HYPHEN is itself a word
#: boundary. `\bmergeable\b` was given its boundaries to stop "unmergeable" matching
#: at offset 2, and it does — but in "non-mergeable" the hyphen satisfies the leading
#: `\b`, so the phrase matches anyway, and `_NEGATORS` holds no `non`/`un` to catch it.
#: "non-mergeable until #182 lands" and "un-clean after the rebase" therefore read as
#: assertions of readiness, on the exact words the boundary fix was written for.
#:
#: Anchored to the END of the preceding text rather than added to `_NEGATORS`, and
#: that is the whole point: `_NEGATORS` is searched across the phrase's entire clause,
#: so a bare `non` there would silence "non-blocking and mergeable" — "non-trivial",
#: "non-fatal", "non-blocking" are ordinary words in these notes and none of them
#: negates a readiness claim six words later. Only a prefix glued to the phrase does.
_PREFIX_NEGATOR = re.compile(r"\b(?:non|un)-$", re.I)

#: How far back to look at all — a backstop against a very long clause dragging in
#: a `not` from thirty words away, applied before the clause split.
_NEGATION_REACH = 60


def _negated(before: str) -> bool:
    """Is a readiness phrase preceded, in its OWN clause, by something reversing it?"""
    if _PREFIX_NEGATOR.search(before):
        return True
    return bool(_NEGATORS.search(_CLAUSE_BREAK.split(before)[-1]))

#: "PR #216" and nothing looser. An item whose ref is an issue can still be
#: ABOUT a pull request — "Merge PR #216 — the panel found 22" is a real plan
#: row — and that PR is what its note's readiness claim is about. A bare `#216`
#: is not enough: on this board most of them are issues, and resolving one as a
#: PR would check the wrong thing and report the answer with a straight face.
_PR_IN_TEXT = re.compile(r"\bPRs?\s*#\s*(\d+)", re.I)

#: An outcome somebody recorded that CLEARS a finding still being raised. A note
#: saying "clean" is defensible when every open chain has been refuted by the
#: person who went and looked, so those do not contradict it. `deferred` and
#: `superseded` are not here: both leave the defect standing.
_CLEARING_OUTCOMES = ("fixed", "refuted")


def _utcnow() -> datetime:
    return datetime.now(UTC)


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Finding:
    """One disagreement, with the facts it was drawn from.

    ``evidence`` is not decoration. Every condition here is a comparison between
    two sources, and a report that states the conclusion without the pair leaves
    a reader to re-run the query to find out whether to believe it.
    """

    condition: str
    subject: str
    summary: str
    repo: str | None = None
    ref: str | None = None
    item_id: str | None = None
    rank: int | None = None
    evidence: dict = field(default_factory=dict)


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Unknown:
    """A check that could not be made, and why.

    Never folded in with the findings. "I looked and found nothing" and "I could
    not look" are different answers, and the whole complaint behind #255 is
    consumers that report the second as the first.
    """

    condition: str
    subject: str
    reason: str
    repo: str | None = None
    ref: str | None = None


@dataclass
class Report:
    generated: str
    repos: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    items_checked: int = 0
    prs_checked: int = 0
    findings: list[Finding] = field(default_factory=list)
    unknowns: list[Unknown] = field(default_factory=list)
    #: Open PRs deliberately not compared against the plan, each with its reason.
    #: Neither a finding nor an unknown — nothing went unchecked and nothing
    #: disagrees — but never silent either: "no untracked PRs" and "no untracked
    #: PRs among the ones I looked at" are different sentences.
    prs_skipped: list[dict] = field(default_factory=list)
    #: Plan scopes with no forge binding, so there was nothing to compare (#323).
    #:
    #: **The third state, and it is not the second one softened.** `unknowns` means
    #: "I could not look"; `findings` means "I looked and the two disagree"; this
    #: means there was never a second source. A `project:` scope has no repository
    #: behind it, so there are no PRs, no issue states and no CI runs anywhere for
    #: the plan to disagree with — not none *found*, none *existing*. Reporting
    #: that as `COULD NOT CHECK` put a permanent unresolvable warning on a
    #: fifteen-minute timer, on a surface whose entire value is that it is
    #: trustworthy; a surface that cries wolf every quarter of an hour stops being
    #: trustworthy in about a day.
    #:
    #: It does not raise `exit_code` and does not clear `complete`, for the same
    #: reason `prs_skipped` does neither: nothing went unchecked. Said once per
    #: tick, in one line, so it is not silent either.
    scopes_skipped: list[dict] = field(default_factory=list)
    #: Items `--apply` completed: one entry per write the board accepted.
    applied: list[dict] = field(default_factory=list)
    #: Writes `--apply` decided on and could not make. Never folded in with
    #: :attr:`applied`, and the reason is this file's own thesis pointed at its own
    #: new limb: "I completed three items" and "I completed two and a third refused"
    #: are different sentences, and on a fifteen-minute timer the second one told as
    #: the first is a plan a reader believes is being maintained.
    apply_failed: list[dict] = field(default_factory=list)
    #: `done_candidate` findings `--apply` will not act on, each with the reason.
    #: Said out loud on every tick rather than left implicit, because this is where
    #: the limb that was NOT shipped lives (#396's issue-ref items) and a residue
    #: nobody names is a residue nobody clears.
    apply_declined: list[dict] = field(default_factory=list)

    @property
    def exit_code(self) -> int:
        """0 clean, 1 partial. Findings do not raise it — they are the output.

        A refused WRITE does raise it. `--apply` is not a check, so a failure there
        is not an unknown and must not be filed as one; but "ran, and part of what
        it decided to do did not happen" is exactly what exit 1 means to the shipped
        unit (`SuccessExitStatus=0 1`, retried on the next tick), and an actor whose
        writes can fail silently under exit 0 is the report-that-never-ran collapse
        this file exists to prevent, arriving through the half that acts.
        """
        return 1 if self.unknowns or self.apply_failed else 0

    def as_dict(self) -> dict:
        return {
            "generated": self.generated,
            "repos": self.repos,
            "items_checked": self.items_checked,
            "prs_checked": self.prs_checked,
            "counts": {c: sum(1 for f in self.findings if f.condition == c) for c in CONDITIONS},
            "findings": [asdict(f) for f in self.findings],
            # Beside the findings and never merged into them; a consumer that
            # reads only `findings` and calls the plan reconciled is the bug.
            "unknowns": [asdict(u) for u in self.unknowns],
            "prs_skipped": list(self.prs_skipped),
            "scopes_skipped": list(self.scopes_skipped),
            "applied": list(self.applied),
            "apply_failed": list(self.apply_failed),
            "apply_declined": list(self.apply_declined),
            # Still about the CHECKS, and deliberately not about the writes. #232's
            # orderer reads this to decide whether the report describes the whole
            # plan; a write that was refused leaves every comparison in it exactly as
            # good as it was. `apply_failed` is what says a write did not happen, and
            # `exit_code` is what makes a caller notice.
            "complete": not self.unknowns,
        }


# ---- the plan's own shape ----------------------------------------------------


def item_ref(item: dict) -> tuple[str | None, str | None]:
    """``("pr", "182")`` for an item that points at something, ``(None, None)`` else."""
    ref = item.get("ref") or {}
    kind, value = ref.get("kind"), ref.get("value")
    if not kind or value in (None, ""):
        return None, None
    return str(kind), str(value).strip().lstrip("#").strip() or None


def item_label(item: dict) -> str:
    """How a finding names an item: its rank and its ref, which is what a reader
    then types. The title is in the summary; the handle belongs here."""
    kind, value = item_ref(item)
    ref = f" {kind}#{value}" if kind and value else ""
    return f"rank {item.get('rank')}{ref}"


def pr_in_text(*texts: str | None) -> str | None:
    """The first ``PR #<n>`` in any of ``texts`` — see :data:`_PR_IN_TEXT`.

    First-wins is right for the question :func:`item_pr` asks ("which PR is this
    note making a claim about"); it is wrong for the question condition 5 asks, and
    :func:`prs_in_text` is that one.
    """
    for text in texts:
        if not text:
            continue
        match = _PR_IN_TEXT.search(text)
        if match:
            return match.group(1)
    return None


def prs_in_text(*texts: str | None) -> list[str]:
    """EVERY ``PR #<n>`` in ``texts``, in the order they appear.

    Which PRs an item accounts for is not the same question as which one its note
    is about, and first-wins answers it wrongly in the one direction that costs:
    an item titled "Land PR #190 and PR #191" tracks both, and reading only #190
    leaves #191 reported as work the plan does not account for — on a board post,
    every tick, forever. Its caller passes the title and not the note; see
    :func:`untracked_prs` for why the two are not the same evidence.
    """
    found: list[str] = []
    for text in texts:
        for number in _PR_IN_TEXT.findall(text or ""):
            if number not in found:
                found.append(number)
    return found


def item_pr(item: dict) -> str | None:
    """The pull request this item's note would be making a claim about.

    Its own ref when that is a PR; otherwise a ``PR #n`` named in the title or
    the note. An issue-backed item saying "Merge PR #216" is making a claim about
    216 and nothing else, and that is the claim /review/findings can answer.
    """
    kind, value = item_ref(item)
    if kind == "pr" and value:
        return value
    return pr_in_text(item.get("title"), item.get("note"))


# ---- condition 1 and 2: the item outlived its work --------------------------


def ref_verdict(kind: str, state: dict) -> tuple[str | None, str, bool]:
    """``(condition_or_None, how_it_reads, was_it_readable)`` for one resolved ref.

    The third element is what keeps "GitHub says this item is fine" apart from
    "GitHub said something this pass does not understand". It is returned as a
    flag rather than left to be recovered from the prose, because the caller used
    to sniff `how_it_reads` for a prefix — so rewording a message silently turned
    an unknown into a clean bill, which is the exact class of bug this file exists
    to report on.

    ``state`` is what GitHub said: ``{"state": "MERGED", "state_reason": None}``.
    A ``condition`` of None means the ref agrees with an open item.

    A closed issue that names no ``stateReason`` still says the item outlived its
    work — the ONE thing an open plan row claims is false — so it is reported as
    a ``done_candidate`` whose evidence records that GitHub did not say which
    kind of closed it was. Withholding the finding over the second question would
    let the first go unreported, and the second is the human's to answer anyway.
    """
    raw = (state.get("state") or "").strip().upper()
    reason = (state.get("state_reason") or "").strip().upper() or None

    if kind == "pr":
        if raw == "MERGED":
            return "done_candidate", "merged", True
        if raw == "CLOSED":
            return "dropped_candidate", "closed without merging", True
        if raw == "OPEN":
            return None, "open", True
        return None, f"an unrecognised state ({raw or 'none'})", False

    if kind == "issue":
        if raw == "CLOSED":
            if reason == "NOT_PLANNED":
                return "dropped_candidate", "closed as not planned", True
            if reason == "COMPLETED":
                return "done_candidate", "closed as completed", True
            return "done_candidate", "closed (GitHub stated no reason)", True
        if raw == "OPEN":
            return None, "open", True
        return None, f"an unrecognised state ({raw or 'none'})", False

    return None, f"a ref kind this pass cannot resolve ({kind})", False


# ---- condition 3: the claim does not describe the present -------------------


#: What :func:`claim_verdict` can conclude. `unknown` is the one this had to grow.
CLAIM_HELD, CLAIM_STALE, CLAIM_UNKNOWN = "held", "stale", "unknown"

#: **An absent lease is not evidence that a claim is dead, and the two TTLs are
#: why.** A plan claim's default TTL is 3600s (`app/api/claims.py`); a lease's is
#: 300s by API default and every live lease on this board runs 1800s, renewed by
#: the lifecycle hook per PROMPT. `/active` lists only leases with `expires_at >
#: now` (`app/api/subagents.py`), so an agent in a single long autonomous turn —
#: the normal shape of the loops this harness drives — drops out of `/active` for
#: up to half an hour while its claim is perfectly live, and there is nothing in
#: the payload to tell "quiet" from "gone". Reported as stale, that is a finding
#: accusing a working agent of holding a dead claim, re-posted every fifteen
#: minutes. What CAN be read is the claim's own `expires`, and while that holds the
#: board's own passive expiry is what will settle the question — an hour at the
#: outside — so this says so instead of guessing.
_LEASE_ASYMMETRY = ("a lease is renewed per prompt and runs 30 minutes against a claim's "
                    "hour, so a single long autonomous turn drops a working agent out of "
                    "/active; the board's own passive expiry settles this at the claim's "
                    "own TTL")


def _claim_live(claim: dict, now: datetime) -> bool | None:
    """Is the claim inside its own TTL? ``None`` = the board did not say."""
    raw = (claim.get("expires") or "").strip()
    if not raw:
        return None
    try:
        expires = datetime.fromisoformat(raw)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    if expires.tzinfo is None:
        expires = expires.replace(tzinfo=UTC)
    return expires > now


def claim_verdict(claim: dict, live_sessions: set[str], live_holders: set[str],
                  now: datetime | None = None) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """``(verdict, why)`` for a live claim on an open item — one of :data:`CLAIM_HELD`,
    :data:`CLAIM_STALE`, :data:`CLAIM_UNKNOWN`.

    The board expires a claim passively: a dead holder stops renewing and the row
    lapses with nobody reaping it. That covers the holder DYING. It does not
    cover the holder still being there while the conversation that took the claim
    is gone — a `/new` or a `/clear` resets the conversation, the seat identity
    and its claims are pinned to the pane, and the lifecycle hook renews the
    lease on every prompt regardless of what the new conversation is about. The
    claim then looks maximally fresh precisely because the agent is busy, with
    something else, and it cannot expire while the pane lives.

    So the session is checked first and the holder second: a claim's ``session``
    and a lease's ``session`` are the same identifier, verified against a live
    board (a healthy claim's session is among the live ones; the orphan's is
    not). A claim naming NO session — one taken by hand through the CLI — can
    only be checked by holder name, and holder names are recycled when an agent
    finishes, so a live name is not proof the holder is the one that claimed it.

    The verdict is three-valued rather than a boolean because the case where
    NOTHING the claim names is in `/active` is the one this pass cannot settle: see
    :data:`_LEASE_ASYMMETRY`. Only one case is a finding, and it is the one passive
    expiry can never reach — the holder is demonstrably live, so its lease is being
    renewed and its absence explains nothing.
    """
    session = (claim.get("session") or "").strip() or None
    holder = (claim.get("holder") or "").strip() or None
    at = now or _utcnow()

    # The session that took the claim is here. Nothing to report.
    if session and session in live_sessions:
        return CLAIM_HELD, "held by a live session"

    # The holder is here and the session that took the claim is not — the reset
    # conversation. The only case where an absence proves something.
    if session and holder and holder in live_holders:
        return CLAIM_STALE, (f"{holder} is live, but the session that took this claim "
                             f"({clip(session, 8)}) is not — a reset conversation still "
                             "holding it, which cannot expire while the pane lives")

    if not session and holder and holder in live_holders:
        return CLAIM_UNKNOWN, ("the claim names no session, so it can only be checked by "
                               "holder name — and names are recycled when an agent "
                               "finishes, so a live name is not proof this holder is the "
                               "one that claimed it")

    # Nothing the claim names is in /active at all. `_claim_live` is three-valued
    # here and every branch below is one of its three answers: a bare `if` would
    # collapse "the board did not say when this expires" into "it expired", and
    # report a comparison this pass never made under a sentence asserting it did.
    absent = (f"nothing in /active names the holder ({holder or 'unnamed'})"
              + (f" or the session that took this claim ({clip(session, 8)})"
                 if session else ""))
    live = _claim_live(claim, at)
    if live:
        return CLAIM_UNKNOWN, (
            f"{absent}, but the claim itself is still inside its own TTL — "
            + _LEASE_ASYMMETRY)
    if live is None:
        # No `expires`, or one that would not parse. The claim cannot vouch for
        # itself and nothing else can either, so this is the second unknown rather
        # than a finding: the pass has no fact that says the claim is dead.
        return CLAIM_UNKNOWN, (
            f"{absent}, and the claim carries no readable `expires` "
            f"({claim.get('expires') or 'none'!r}), so whether it is still inside "
            "its own TTL could not be checked either")
    return CLAIM_STALE, (f"neither the holder ({holder or 'unnamed'}) nor the session that "
                         f"took this claim ({clip(session, 8) if session else 'none named'}) "
                         "is live, and the claim is past its own expiry")


# ---- condition 4: the note is fiction ---------------------------------------


def note_asserts_ready(note: str | None) -> str | None:
    """The phrase by which a note claims its PR is ready, or None.

    Every match is checked for a negator in front of it, and a negated one is
    skipped rather than ending the search: "not ready to land, but the panel is
    clean" makes a readiness claim in its second clause, and stopping at the first
    match would miss it while stopping at the first *un-negated* one finds it.
    """
    if not note:
        return None
    for match in _READY.finditer(note):
        before = note[max(0, match.start() - _NEGATION_REACH):match.start()]
        if not _negated(before):
            return match.group(0)
    return None


def _clears(finding: dict) -> bool:
    """Did somebody go and look, and settle this finding?"""
    outcome = finding.get("outcome") or {}
    return (outcome.get("outcome") or "") in _CLEARING_OUTCOMES


#: Verdicts a master judge never actually pronounced. `unjudged` is what the panel
#: writes when the judge crashed or never ran (`app/api/reviews.py`), and `sonar` is
#: the hard gate's own issues, which no judge rules on either.
_UNADJUDICATED = ("unjudged", "sonar")


def _unadjudicated(chain: dict) -> bool:
    """Was this chain's latest observation never ruled on by a judge?

    `GET /review/findings` sets `status: "dismissed"` only when EVERY verdict in the
    chain is `dismissed`, so a chain whose latest observation is `unjudged` or
    `sonar` comes back `open` — and counting it as "still raised in the latest
    round" is defensible, but reporting it without saying so is not. The endpoint's
    own scoring excludes these from both sides of its measurement; a denial that
    rests entirely on findings nobody confirmed has to say how many.
    """
    observations = chain.get("observations") or []
    return bool(observations) and observations[-1].get("verdict") in _UNADJUDICATED


def findings_denial(history: dict) -> tuple[str | None, str | None, dict]:
    """``(denial, unknown_reason, evidence)`` — does the review record deny readiness?

    Two independent signals, in this order:

    **Findings still raised.** A chain with ``status: "open"`` was raised in the
    PR's most recent round. That is window-independent for the newest run, so a
    ``truncated`` history does not weaken it. Chains whose recorded outcome is
    ``fixed`` or ``refuted`` are excluded: somebody went and looked.

    **The cycle has not stopped.** Only when it can be attributed, and this is
    where the board's own three-state discipline has to be honoured rather than
    read for truthiness. ``stopped`` is null when no attributable cycle said,
    which is not false; and the summary only speaks for the PR when ``cycles <=
    1 and not truncated``. A board too old to report ``cycles`` at all cannot
    have its summary attributed — the deployed v2.48 is exactly that — so this
    returns an unknown instead of reading the field anyway.
    """
    findings = history.get("findings") or []
    open_chains = [f for f in findings if f.get("status") == "open"]
    standing = [f for f in open_chains if not _clears(f)]
    cleared = len(open_chains) - len(standing)
    unadjudicated = sum(1 for f in standing if _unadjudicated(f))
    truncated = bool(history.get("truncated"))
    evidence = {
        "rounds": history.get("rounds"),
        "open_findings": len(standing),
        "open_findings_cleared_by_outcome": cleared,
        "open_findings_unadjudicated": unadjudicated,
        "stopped": history.get("stopped"),
        "stop_reason": history.get("stop_reason"),
        "cycles": history.get("cycles"),
        "truncated": truncated,
    }

    if standing:
        worst = sorted(f.get("severity") or "P9" for f in standing)[0]
        # The count is in the denial and not only in the evidence, because a
        # denial nobody can weigh is a denial a reader has to go and re-derive.
        unruled = (f", {unadjudicated} of them never adjudicated"
                   if unadjudicated else "")
        return (f"{len(standing)} finding(s) still raised in the latest round "
                f"(worst {worst}{unruled})", None, evidence)

    if "cycles" not in history:
        return None, ("the board reported no `cycles`, so its stop summary cannot be "
                      "attributed to one cycle (a board older than v2.54)"), evidence

    cycles = history.get("cycles")
    if not isinstance(cycles, int) or cycles > 1 or truncated:
        return None, (f"the stop summary does not speak for this PR "
                      f"(cycles={cycles}, truncated={truncated})"), evidence

    stopped = history.get("stopped")
    if stopped is None:
        return None, "no attributable cycle said whether it stopped", evidence
    if stopped is False:
        reason = history.get("stop_reason") or "no reason given"
        return f"the review cycle has not stopped: {reason}", None, evidence
    return None, None, evidence


# ---- condition 5: work the plan does not account for ------------------------


#: Logins whose pull requests are already somebody else's job. The harness ships a
#: whole loop for dependabot's (`harness/loops/lander.py`, `DEPENDABOT_LOGIN`), and
#: those PRs are deliberately never on the plan — so on any repo with dependabot
#: enabled every one of them is an `untracked_pr`, permanently, in a post made every
#: fifteen minutes, drowning the findings a reader is here for. `gh` reports
#: `author.is_bot`, which catches renovate and the rest without a list; this list is
#: the fallback for a `gh` that does not say.
BOT_LOGINS = ("dependabot[bot]", "renovate[bot]", "github-actions[bot]")


def pr_author(pr: dict) -> str:
    return ((pr.get("author") or {}).get("login") or "").strip()


def is_bot_pr(pr: dict) -> bool:
    return bool((pr.get("author") or {}).get("is_bot")) or pr_author(pr) in BOT_LOGINS


def untracked_prs(open_prs: list[dict], items: list[dict],
                  include_drafts: bool = False) -> tuple[list[dict], list[Unknown], list[dict]]:
    """``(untracked, could-not-tell, deliberately-skipped)`` over one repo's open PRs.

    ``items`` here is every plan row, open or finished — see :func:`reconcile`'s
    ``tracking_items``. A row that named a PR still names it after it is marked done.

    A PR is accounted for three ways, and all three are needed: an item whose ref
    IS it, an item whose issue ref is one this PR closes, or an item whose TITLE
    names it as ``PR #n``. Only the first is a bare number match — the second is
    what stops "Rebase and land PR #182 (#163)" and its issue-backed sibling from
    both reading as untracked work. ALL the ``PR #n``s a title names count, not
    the first: "Land PR #190 and PR #191" tracks two.

    **The note is deliberately not read for this, and the ref/title pair is why.**
    An item's ref and its title say what the item IS; its note is prose about the
    work, and prose mentions a PR without owning it all the time — "follows PR
    #999", "rebase after PR #182 lands", "blocked until PR #247 lands" are the
    shape of real notes on this board. Reading those as ownership marks a PR
    tracked that nothing on the plan is doing, and condition 5 then goes silent
    about it on every tick forever. That is a false negative on the one condition
    whose entire job is finding unaccounted-for work, which is the direction this
    file's own thesis says costs the most. A PR genuinely owned by an issue-backed
    item is already reached by the issue leg, which is what that leg is for;
    :func:`item_pr` still reads the note, because "which PR is this note making a
    claim about" is a different question and prose answers it correctly.

    A PR whose ``closingIssuesReferences`` GitHub did not return cannot have the
    second leg checked, so it comes back as an unknown rather than a finding: the
    whole point of this pass is not to report an unmade check as a clean one.

    The third list is what was not compared and why — bot PRs (see
    :data:`BOT_LOGINS`) and, unless asked for, drafts. It is returned rather than
    dropped because this file's rule is that no cap and no filter is silent: the
    report says what it left out, so a reader can tell "nothing untracked" from
    "nothing untracked among the PRs I looked at".
    """
    # Keyed by (repo, number), never by number alone. Two repos both have a #12,
    # and a plan item tracking one repo's #12 would otherwise account for the
    # other's — hiding real untracked work behind a coincidence of numbering. The
    # same rule `issue_key` in qbdata.py states for claims, for the same reason.
    tracked_prs: set[tuple[str, str]] = set()
    tracked_issues: set[tuple[str, str]] = set()
    for item in items:
        repo = item.get("repo")
        if not repo:
            # A fleet-scoped item names no repo, so it cannot be said to track a
            # PR in one. Attributing it to every repo is how the bug above gets in.
            continue
        kind, value = item_ref(item)
        if kind == "pr" and value:
            tracked_prs.add((repo, value))
        elif kind == "issue" and value:
            tracked_issues.add((repo, value))
        # Title only — see the docstring. A note naming a PR is talking about it,
        # not accounting for it.
        for named in prs_in_text(item.get("title")):
            tracked_prs.add((repo, named))

    findings: list[dict] = []
    unknowns: list[Unknown] = []
    skipped: list[dict] = []
    for pr in open_prs:
        number = str(pr.get("number"))
        repo = pr.get("repo") or ""
        if (repo, number) in tracked_prs:
            continue
        if is_bot_pr(pr):
            skipped.append({"number": pr.get("number"), "repo": pr.get("repo"),
                            "reason": f"authored by {pr_author(pr) or 'a bot'}, which the "
                                      "plan is not expected to track"})
            continue
        if pr.get("isDraft") and not include_drafts:
            skipped.append({"number": pr.get("number"), "repo": pr.get("repo"),
                            "reason": "a draft, which is not yet work the plan owes an "
                                      "item (--include-drafts to compare them too)"})
            continue
        closes = pr.get("closingIssuesReferences")
        if closes is None:
            unknowns.append(Unknown(
                condition="untracked_pr",
                subject=f"PR #{number}",
                repo=pr.get("repo"),
                ref=f"pr#{number}",
                reason="`gh` returned no closingIssuesReferences, so whether a plan "
                       "item tracks this PR through its issue could not be checked",
            ))
            continue
        if any((repo, str(i.get("number"))) in tracked_issues for i in closes):
            continue
        findings.append(pr)
    return findings, unknowns, skipped


# ---- the pass ---------------------------------------------------------------


def reconcile(items: list[dict], ref_states: dict[tuple[str, str, str], dict | None],
              live_sessions: set[str], live_holders: set[str],
              histories: dict[tuple[str, str], dict | None],
              open_prs: list[dict], repos: list[str],
              now: datetime | None = None,
              ref_errors: dict[tuple[str, str, str], str] | None = None,
              include_drafts: bool = False,
              tracking_items: list[dict] | None = None) -> Report:
    """Every condition over already-fetched data. No I/O, so it is all testable.

    ``ref_states`` is keyed ``(repo, kind, value)`` and ``histories`` ``(repo,
    pr)``; a key present with a ``None`` value means the lookup was attempted and
    failed, which is reported. A key ABSENT means it was never needed.
    ``ref_errors`` carries WHY a lookup failed, under the same key: "no such pull
    request" and "API rate limit exceeded" want opposite responses from a reader,
    and without the reason both arrive as the same unknown.

    ``items`` are the rows CHECKED — conditions 1-4 run on open work only, because
    a finished row pointing at a merged PR is the record working. ``tracking_items``
    are the rows that ACCOUNT for a PR in condition 5, and they are every row the
    plan has, defaulting to ``items`` when a caller has no wider list.

    **The two are different lists, and collapsing them is a bug in whichever
    direction it is done.** The repo scope is drawn from the whole plan (see
    :func:`run`) precisely so a repo whose rows are all finished still gets its open
    PRs read — but if only the OPEN rows can account for a PR, nothing in such a
    repo can account for anything, and every one of its open PRs becomes an
    `untracked_pr` finding on every tick forever. A repo with three done rows from
    last month and a dozen live PRs nobody put on the plan is then a dozen standing
    findings: the drowning-in-noise problem the bot and draft filters exist to
    prevent, let back in through the widened scope. A plan row that named a PR still
    names it after it is marked done; "the plan accounts for this" does not expire.
    """
    at = now or _utcnow()
    errors = ref_errors or {}
    accounting = items if tracking_items is None else tracking_items
    report = Report(generated=at.isoformat(), repos=sorted(repos),
                    items_checked=len(items), prs_checked=len(open_prs))

    for item in items:
        repo = item.get("repo")
        kind, value = item_ref(item)
        label = item_label(item)
        title = clip(item.get("title"), 70)

        # 1 + 2 — the item against its work.
        if kind and value:
            ref = f"{kind}#{value}"
            state = ref_states.get((repo or "", kind, value), "missing")
            if not forge_repo(repo):
                # The item's scope names no repository, so there is nothing to ask
                # about its ref. Said plainly rather than reported as a failed
                # lookup: nothing went wrong, the item is simply not answerable.
                #
                # Two scopes reach this and one sentence covers both. A fleet item
                # has named no repo since the column existed. A `project:` item
                # cannot legitimately carry an `issue` or `pr` ref at all — the
                # board refuses one (#323) — so a row here is a real disagreement
                # about a row that predates the refusal, and an unknown is what it
                # is. The scope being skipped elsewhere in this report is about
                # scopes; this is about one row inside one.
                report.unknowns.append(Unknown(
                    condition="ref_unresolved", subject=label, repo=repo, ref=ref,
                    reason=f"the item's scope names no repository, so {ref} cannot "
                           "be resolved against any GitHub project"))
            elif state == "missing" or state is None:
                why = errors.get((repo, kind, value))
                report.unknowns.append(Unknown(
                    condition="ref_unresolved", subject=label, repo=repo, ref=ref,
                    reason=f"{ref} could not be resolved against GitHub, so whether "
                           "this item has outlived its work is unknown"
                           + (f" — {why}" if why else "")))
            else:
                condition, reads, readable = ref_verdict(kind, state)
                if condition:
                    report.findings.append(Finding(
                        condition=condition, subject=label, repo=repo, ref=ref,
                        item_id=item.get("item_id"), rank=item.get("rank"),
                        summary=f"open item, but {ref} is {reads} — {title}",
                        evidence={"item_state": item.get("state"),
                                  "github_state": state.get("state"),
                                  "github_state_reason": state.get("state_reason"),
                                  "url": state.get("url"),
                                  "idle_days": item.get("idle_days"),
                                  "stale": item.get("stale")}))
                elif not readable:
                    report.unknowns.append(Unknown(
                        condition="ref_unresolved", subject=label, repo=repo, ref=ref,
                        reason=f"GitHub reported {reads} for {ref}"))

        # 3 — the claim against who is actually here.
        claim = item.get("claim")
        if claim:
            verdict, why = claim_verdict(claim, live_sessions, live_holders, now=at)
            if verdict == CLAIM_STALE:
                report.findings.append(Finding(
                    condition="stale_claim", subject=label, repo=repo,
                    ref=f"{kind}#{value}" if kind and value else None,
                    item_id=item.get("item_id"), rank=item.get("rank"),
                    summary=f"claimed, but the claim is stale: {why} — {title}",
                    evidence={"holder": claim.get("holder"),
                              "session": claim.get("session"),
                              "claim_id": claim.get("claim_id"),
                              "acquired": claim.get("acquired"),
                              "expires": claim.get("expires"),
                              "note": clip(claim.get("note"), 120)}))
            elif verdict == CLAIM_UNKNOWN:
                report.unknowns.append(Unknown(
                    condition="stale_claim", subject=label, repo=repo,
                    ref=f"{kind}#{value}" if kind and value else None,
                    reason=f"the claim held by {claim.get('holder')} could not be "
                           f"checked: {why}"))

        # 4 — the note against the review record.
        pr = item_pr(item)
        phrase = note_asserts_ready(item.get("note"))
        if pr and phrase:
            history = histories.get((repo or "", pr), "missing")
            if history == "missing" or history is None:
                report.unknowns.append(Unknown(
                    condition="note_contradicted", subject=label, repo=repo, ref=f"pr#{pr}",
                    reason=f"the note claims {phrase!r} but /review/findings for PR #{pr} "
                           "could not be read, so the claim is unchecked"))
            else:
                denial, unknown, evidence = findings_denial(history)
                if denial:
                    report.findings.append(Finding(
                        condition="note_contradicted", subject=label, repo=repo,
                        ref=f"pr#{pr}", item_id=item.get("item_id"), rank=item.get("rank"),
                        summary=f"the note says {phrase!r}, the board says {denial}",
                        evidence={"phrase": phrase, "note": clip(item.get("note"), 200),
                                  **evidence}))
                elif unknown:
                    report.unknowns.append(Unknown(
                        condition="note_contradicted", subject=label, repo=repo,
                        ref=f"pr#{pr}",
                        reason=f"the note claims {phrase!r}; {unknown}"))

    # 5 — work nothing on the plan accounts for.
    untracked, pr_unknowns, skipped = untracked_prs(open_prs, accounting, include_drafts)
    report.unknowns.extend(pr_unknowns)
    report.prs_skipped.extend(skipped)
    for pr in untracked:
        number = pr.get("number")
        report.findings.append(Finding(
            condition="untracked_pr", subject=f"PR #{number}", repo=pr.get("repo"),
            ref=f"pr#{number}",
            summary=f"open PR no plan item accounts for — {clip(pr.get('title'), 70)}",
            evidence={"draft": bool(pr.get("isDraft")), "url": pr.get("url"),
                      "updated": pr.get("updatedAt"),
                      "closes": [i.get("number") for i in (pr.get("closingIssuesReferences") or [])]}))
    return report


# ---- fetching ---------------------------------------------------------------


class Unavailable(RuntimeError):
    """The pass cannot run at all — reported as exit 2, never as a traceback."""


def _gh_json(args: list[str]) -> tuple[object | None, str | None]:
    """``(parsed JSON, why not)`` for one `gh` call.

    The reason is half the answer, and throwing it away made every failure the same
    unhelpful unknown. "no such pull request" is a plan item pointing at a ref that
    does not exist — a real, permanent disagreement somebody should go and fix —
    while "API rate limit exceeded" is this tick's weather; they want opposite
    responses from a reader, and the whole thesis of this pass is that the reason a
    check could not be made is part of the report.
    """
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(["gh", *args], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=GH_TIMEOUT)
    except FileNotFoundError as exc:
        raise Unavailable("`gh` is not on PATH — every GitHub-side check would be "
                          "an unknown, which is not a report worth printing") from exc
    except Exception as exc:                                # noqa: BLE001
        return None, f"the `gh` call did not complete ({type(exc).__name__}: {clip(str(exc), 60)})"
    if out.returncode != 0:
        return None, (clip(out.stderr, 120) or f"`gh` exited {out.returncode} and said nothing")
    try:
        return json.loads(out.stdout), None
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        return None, "`gh` returned something that is not JSON"


#: A repository name `gh --repo` will take and this pass will put in a URL. Board
#: text is free — `ItemIn.repo` is 256 characters of anything and `ItemIn.ref_value`
#: 64, normalised only by stripping a leading `#` — and `gh` reads an argument
#: beginning with `-` as a FLAG, not as a value. A ref spelled `--repo=someone/else`
#: would silently answer the question about a different repository, and the verdict
#: would then be reported as a checked fact, which is the one thing this pass
#: promises never to do. So both halves are validated before either reaches a
#: subprocess or a query string, and a value that fails is reported as the unknown
#: it is.
_REPO_OK = re.compile(r"\A[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*/[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*\Z")

#: The sigil `app/scope.py` gives a plan scope with no forge behind it (#323).
#: Pinned here rather than imported: the harness does not import from `app/` (it
#: talks to the board over HTTP and ships separately), and the spelling is part of
#: the wire format either way.
NON_FORGE_SIGIL = "project:"


def forge_repo(scope: str | None) -> str | None:
    """The repository this plan scope is bound to, or None if it is bound to none.

    **This is the question, and asking it the other way round is what produced the
    noise #323 is about.** `fetch_open_prs` asked "why can I not ask `gh` about
    this?", which presumes the answer should be yes and so reports every no as a
    failure. A plan scope is a name for a body of work; most of them name a GitHub
    repo *as well*, and that binding is what there is to reconcile against. A scope
    with none — `project:65lowther`, the fleet scope — is not a failed lookup and
    not a lookup at all.

    Binding is decided by the spelling and **not** by whether the bound name is
    usable, and keeping those two apart is the whole point. `65lowther` is a scope
    that claims to name a repository and names an unusable one: still a bound
    scope, still `gh`'s to refuse, still an honest `COULD NOT CHECK` from
    :func:`fetch_open_prs` with the reason it always gave. Only NULL (the fleet,
    which has named no repository since the column existed) and the sigil are
    bound to nothing — and both of those say so, rather than being inferred from a
    failed match, which is exactly the inference #323 forbids.
    """
    if not scope or str(scope).lower().startswith(NON_FORGE_SIGIL):
        return None
    return str(scope)


def scope_label(scope: str) -> str:
    """What a scope is called in the report. `project:65lowther` -> `65lowther`.

    `short_repo` drops the owner because it never distinguishes two repos anybody
    is looking at; the sigil is the same kind of noise on a line a person reads,
    and the same kind of load-bearing detail everywhere else.
    """
    if str(scope).lower().startswith(NON_FORGE_SIGIL):
        return str(scope)[len(NON_FORGE_SIGIL):]
    return short_repo(scope)


def fetch_ref_state(repo: str, kind: str, value: str) -> tuple[dict | None, str | None]:
    """``(what GitHub says about one ref, why it could not be asked)``."""
    if kind == "pr":
        fields = "number,state,isDraft,mergedAt,closedAt,title,url"
    elif kind == "issue":
        fields = "number,state,stateReason,title,url"
    else:
        return None, f"`gh` has no view command for a {kind!r} ref"
    if not value.isdigit():
        return None, (f"the ref value {clip(value, 40)!r} is not a number, so it does not "
                      "name a pull request or an issue `gh` could be asked about")
    if not _REPO_OK.match(repo):
        return None, f"{clip(repo, 40)!r} is not a repository name `gh` can be asked about"
    got, problem = _gh_json([kind, "view", value, "--repo", repo, "--json", fields])
    if not isinstance(got, dict):
        return None, problem or "`gh` did not return an object"
    return {"state": got.get("state"), "state_reason": got.get("stateReason"),
            "url": got.get("url"), "title": got.get("title")}, None


def fetch_open_prs(repo: str) -> tuple[list[dict], Unknown | None]:
    """Open PRs with the linked issues condition 5 needs, and what went wrong.

    A list that hit :data:`PR_FETCH_LIMIT` is reported rather than trimmed
    quietly — a silent cap here reads as "the plan accounts for everything".
    """
    if not _REPO_OK.match(repo):
        return [], Unknown(condition="untracked_pr", subject=clip(repo, 40), repo=repo,
                           reason=f"{clip(repo, 40)!r} is not a repository name `gh` can be "
                                  "asked about, so its open PRs were not compared against "
                                  "the plan at all")
    got, problem = _gh_json(["pr", "list", "--repo", repo, "--state", "open",
                             "--limit", str(PR_FETCH_LIMIT), "--json",
                             "number,title,isDraft,updatedAt,url,author,"
                             "closingIssuesReferences"])
    if not isinstance(got, list):
        return [], Unknown(condition="untracked_pr", subject=short_repo(repo), repo=repo,
                           reason=f"`gh pr list` failed ({problem or 'no reason given'}), so "
                                  "this repo's open PRs were not compared against the plan "
                                  "at all")
    for pr in got:
        pr["repo"] = repo
    if len(got) >= PR_FETCH_LIMIT:
        return got, Unknown(condition="untracked_pr", subject=short_repo(repo), repo=repo,
                            reason=f"the open-PR list hit the {PR_FETCH_LIMIT}-row fetch "
                                   "limit, so there may be untracked PRs beyond it")
    return got, None


def fetch_history(client, repo: str, pr: str) -> dict | None:
    """The board's review record for one PR. Built with `urlencode`, not `f"…{repo}…"`:
    a repo or ref carrying `&` or `=` would otherwise inject extra query parameters
    into the board's own path — the same free board text `_REPO_OK` guards `gh`
    against, arriving at the other source this pass compares."""
    try:
        return client.get("/review/findings?" + urlencode({"repo": repo, "pr": pr}))
    except Exception:                                       # noqa: BLE001
        return None


def fetch_live(client) -> tuple[set[str], set[str]]:
    """(live session ids, live holder identities) from /active."""
    agents = (client.get("/active") or {}).get("agents") or []
    return ({(a.get("session") or "").strip() for a in agents if a.get("session")},
            {(a.get("holder") or "").strip() for a in agents if a.get("holder")})


#: The one board client this process builds, and the reason there is a function for
#: it. `resolve_config` sources the site config in a subshell and can run
#: `QUARTERBACK_TOKEN_CMD` (qbdata.py), so a second call is a second secret-fetch
#: subprocess per tick — and a token command that succeeded for the three reads can
#: fail for the write, producing "report stands, but the board post failed" against
#: a board that answered a second ago.
_CLIENT: tuple | None = None


def client_once():
    """``(client, cfg)``, built at most once per process."""
    global _CLIENT
    if _CLIENT is None:
        _CLIENT = board_client()
    return _CLIENT


def run(repo_filter: str | None = None, include_drafts: bool = False) -> Report:
    """Fetch everything the conditions need, then reconcile.

    ``repo_filter`` is folded to lower case, because the board folds a scope when
    it stores one (``app.api.plan._norm_scope``) and this compares against what the
    board returned. Unfolded, ``--repo PrisonBlues/Quarterback`` matched no row,
    reconciled nothing and printed "the plan agrees with GitHub on everything
    checked" — a clean bill from a pass that never ran, which is the one output
    this pass exists to make impossible.
    """
    repo_filter = repo_filter.strip().lower() if repo_filter else None
    try:
        client, _cfg = client_once()
    except Exception as exc:                                # noqa: BLE001
        raise Unavailable(f"no board to reconcile against: {exc}") from exc

    try:
        # `include_done` because the REPO SET below comes from every row the plan
        # has: see :data:`PLAN_LIMIT`. The item checks still run on open rows only.
        plan = client.get(f"/plan?include_done=true&limit={PLAN_LIMIT}")
    except Exception as exc:                                # noqa: BLE001
        raise Unavailable(f"the board's plan could not be read: {exc}") from exc

    if not isinstance(plan, dict) or "items" not in plan:
        # An answer with no `items` key is not a plan with no items. A proxy page,
        # a 204 from a board mid-deploy, a body that is not an object — read as an
        # empty plan, every one of them yields zero items, zero unknowns, exit 0
        # and the line "The plan agrees with GitHub and the board on everything
        # checked": a clean bill from a check that never ran, which is the exact
        # collapse this pass exists to report on.
        raise Unavailable("the board's plan came back with no `items` key, which is not "
                          "the same as a plan with no items")

    all_items = plan.get("items") or []
    items = [i for i in all_items if i.get("state") == "open"]
    # WHAT the cap cut decides what the truncation unknown may claim. The board
    # orders open work ahead of history (`_scope_items`) and publishes `counts.open`
    # for the whole scope, so the page can say whether any OPEN row was lost — and a
    # report that said "items past the cap were not reconciled" when only finished
    # rows were cut would be overstating its own blindness, which is the same error
    # in the other direction.
    truncated = bool(plan.get("truncated"))
    plan_open = (plan.get("counts") or {}).get("open")
    open_cut = not isinstance(plan_open, int) or len(items) < plan_open
    if repo_filter:
        items = [i for i in items if (i.get("repo") or "") == repo_filter]

    try:
        live_sessions, live_holders = fetch_live(client)
    except Exception as exc:                                # noqa: BLE001
        raise Unavailable(f"/active could not be read, so no claim could be checked "
                          f"against who is here: {exc}") from exc

    # From ALL the plan's rows, not just its open ones. A repo whose items are all
    # done or dropped is still a repo the plan names — and it is the most likely
    # place for untracked work to be, so dropping it from the scope means its open
    # PRs cannot be reported at all, silently, under a report headed "every repo
    # the board's plan names".
    named = sorted({i["repo"] for i in all_items if i.get("repo")})
    scopes = [repo_filter] if repo_filter else named
    # Split before anything reaches `gh`. A scope with no forge binding is not a
    # repo whose lookup failed, so it must not be handed to a function whose only
    # two answers are "here are its PRs" and "I could not ask" (#323). It is
    # reported once, here, as the third thing it is.
    repos = [s for s in scopes if forge_repo(s)]
    # …but only for a scope the PLAN NAMES. `--repo` is an argument, not a fact:
    # `--repo project:65lowthr` would otherwise print "1 scope with nothing to
    # compare" and exit clean, asserting that scope exists on the strength of
    # somebody having typed it — and the registry that would have refused the typo
    # lives on the board, which this pass reads over HTTP and cannot consult. A
    # BOUND scope keeps taking the filter as given, because asking about a
    # repository the plan has no rows for is the legitimate way to find out that
    # none of its open PRs are tracked.
    unbound = [s for s in scopes if not forge_repo(s) and s in set(named)]

    ref_states: dict[tuple[str, str, str], dict | None] = {}
    ref_errors: dict[tuple[str, str, str], str] = {}
    for item in items:
        repo = item.get("repo")
        kind, value = item_ref(item)
        if not (forge_repo(repo) and kind and value):
            continue
        key = (repo, kind, value)
        if key not in ref_states:
            ref_states[key], problem = fetch_ref_state(repo, kind, value)
            if problem:
                ref_errors[key] = problem

    histories: dict[tuple[str, str], dict | None] = {}
    for item in items:
        repo = item.get("repo")
        pr = item_pr(item)
        if not (forge_repo(repo) and pr and note_asserts_ready(item.get("note"))):
            continue
        if (repo, pr) not in histories:
            histories[(repo, pr)] = fetch_history(client, repo, pr)

    open_prs: list[dict] = []
    pr_unknowns: list[Unknown] = []
    for repo in repos:
        rows, problem = fetch_open_prs(repo)
        open_prs.extend(rows)
        if problem:
            pr_unknowns.append(problem)

    # Every row the plan has, under the same repo filter the checked items got.
    # The repo scope came from this list, so what can account for a PR has to as
    # well, or a repo included only for its finished rows reports all of them.
    tracking = all_items
    if repo_filter:
        tracking = [i for i in tracking if (i.get("repo") or "") == repo_filter]

    report = reconcile(items, ref_states, live_sessions, live_holders,
                       histories, open_prs, repos, ref_errors=ref_errors,
                       include_drafts=include_drafts, tracking_items=tracking)
    report.unknowns.extend(pr_unknowns)
    report.scopes_skipped.extend(
        {"scope": scope, "label": scope_label(scope),
         "items": sum(1 for i in items if i.get("repo") == scope),
         "reason": "a project scope: no repo behind it, so there are no PRs, "
                   "issue states or CI runs anywhere for the plan to disagree with"}
        for scope in unbound)
    if truncated:
        # Reported once, for the plan as a whole: the rows past the cap were never
        # read, so there is no subject to attach a per-item unknown to — and no
        # finding condition either, which is why it carries `plan_incomplete`
        # rather than being filed under `done_candidate` while its own text says it
        # invalidates `untracked_pr`.
        report.unknowns.append(Unknown(
            condition="plan_incomplete", subject="the plan",
            reason=f"`GET /plan` reported the list truncated at {PLAN_LIMIT} rows, so "
                   + ("open items past the cap were not reconciled and an "
                      "`untracked_pr` above may be tracked by one of them"
                      if open_cut else
                      "the plan's finished rows past the cap were not read — every open "
                      "item was reconciled, but a repo whose only rows are past the cap "
                      "was never asked for its open PRs")))
    return report


# ---- the actor: the one transition this pass may make (#552) ----------------


#: What `--apply` acts on, spelled as three separate facts rather than one label.
#:
#: The condition alone is not enough and the ref kind alone is not enough, so the
#: write re-reads both off the finding's own evidence rather than trusting the
#: LABEL — even though :func:`ref_verdict` gives a pull request `done_candidate`
#: for `MERGED` and for nothing else today. `CONDITIONS` is a vocabulary five
#: checks share and any of them may legitimately widen; the day a `done_candidate`
#: is raised for a PR GitHub reports some other way, an actor that trusted the
#: label would complete a plan item on a fact nobody checked, silently, on a
#: fifteen-minute timer. So the write reads the state GitHub actually reported, and
#: one it does not recognise is declined and named rather than acted on.
#:
#: **It is not a second call to GitHub, and the guard is not claiming to be one**
#: (PR #719 review). `github_state` is what this pass already fetched, minutes
#: earlier at most, and re-fetching it would buy nothing here: `MERGED` is terminal
#: on GitHub, so the one state acted on cannot have stopped being true in between.
#: What the re-read defends against is this file drifting away from itself — a
#: condition widening upstream of an actor that reads only the condition — which is
#: a change to the code and not a change on the forge.
APPLY_CONDITION = "done_candidate"
APPLY_REF_KIND = "pr"
APPLY_STATE = "MERGED"

#: Why an issue-ref `done_candidate` is left for a human — the limb #552 splits off.
#:
#: A closed issue IS one of that issue's three completion signals, so this is a
#: scoping decision and not a claim that the signal is wrong. What makes the two
#: legs unequal is the `test`-branch flow: `MERGED` is base-branch agnostic, a
#: closing keyword only fires on a merge into the repository's DEFAULT branch, and
#: an item whose ref is an issue cannot see the pull request that implemented it
#: (#396). So in a repo that lands work on an integration branch, "the issue closed"
#: arrives one or two merges after the work did — and an actor keyed on it would be
#: correct on this repo and inert on lexray, which is worse than not shipping,
#: because the plan would then carry something that claims to be handling
#: completion. Declined out loud instead, every tick, with somewhere to read why.
ISSUE_LIMB = ("this pass completes the PR leg only (#552): an item whose ref is an "
              "issue cannot see the PR that implemented it (#396), and a closing "
              "keyword only fires on a merge into the default branch — so a closed "
              "issue is not the same fact as landed work in every repo. Left for a "
              "human or a `plan_done` call")


def apply_note(finding: Finding) -> str:
    """What the completed item's note will say — the fact and where to read it.

    `POST /plan/item/done` appends this to the item's own note rather than
    replacing it, so it is a receipt and says only what a receipt says. The URL is
    in it because "where did this land" is the first question a reader of a done row
    asks, and the pass has GitHub's answer in hand.
    """
    url = (finding.evidence or {}).get("url")
    return (f"{finding.ref} is merged — recorded by qb-reconcile"
            + (f" ({url})" if url else ""))


def _attempt(finding: Finding, **extra) -> dict:
    """The identifying half of an applied / failed / declined entry.

    One shape for all three, so a reader (and the digest) sees the same fields
    whichever list a row is in.
    """
    return {"item_id": finding.item_id, "repo": finding.repo, "ref": finding.ref,
            "rank": finding.rank, "subject": finding.subject, **extra}


def apply_candidates(report: Report) -> tuple[list[Finding], list[dict]]:
    """``(the findings --apply completes, the done candidates it will not)``.

    Pure, and separate from the writing, so the predicate is testable without a
    board — the same reason :func:`reconcile` does no I/O.

    Every `done_candidate` reaches one list or the other. A finding that is neither
    completed nor accounted for is the failure mode this whole issue is about, one
    level in: a pass that quietly acts on some rows and says nothing about the rest
    leaves a residue whose size nobody can read off the report.
    """
    doing: list[Finding] = []
    declined: list[dict] = []
    for finding in report.findings:
        if finding.condition != APPLY_CONDITION:
            continue
        kind, _, _value = (finding.ref or "").partition("#")
        state = str((finding.evidence or {}).get("github_state") or "").strip().upper()
        if kind != APPLY_REF_KIND:
            declined.append(_attempt(finding, reason=(
                ISSUE_LIMB if kind == "issue" else
                f"the ref kind is {kind or 'unstated'!r}, and this pass completes "
                f"{APPLY_REF_KIND!r} refs only")))
        elif state != APPLY_STATE:
            # See APPLY_STATE: the condition said done, GitHub's own word did not
            # say merged, and the two disagreeing is a reason to stop rather than a
            # detail to round off.
            declined.append(_attempt(finding, reason=(
                f"the finding is a {APPLY_CONDITION} but GitHub reports "
                f"{state or 'nothing'} rather than {APPLY_STATE}, so the one fact "
                "this pass acts on was not established")))
        elif not finding.item_id:
            # `untracked_pr` carries no item and never reaches here; a
            # `done_candidate` without one would be a plan row this pass cannot
            # address, and inventing an id is not the answer.
            declined.append(_attempt(finding, reason=(
                "the finding carries no item_id, so there is no plan row to complete")))
        else:
            doing.append(finding)
    return doing, declined


def _already_done(view: dict, before: str) -> str | None:
    """Who had recorded this item done before THIS WRITE went out, if anybody.

    **Two observers of one merge is the endpoint's design, not a race this caller
    invented.** `POST /plan/item/done` says any agent may record a completion —
    "whoever observes it may write it down" — and it appends the note rather than
    replacing it, precisely so two receipts can coexist. `complete_item` only stamps
    `done_by` when the row was not already done, so a second record leaves the first
    holder's name and timestamp in place and adds a note line under it.

    What that leaves is not a wrong state — the row is done either way, which is the
    answer both observers wanted — but a duplicated receipt, and this file's rule is
    that nothing it does is silent. So the pass reads the timestamp it got back and
    compares it against the moment it sent the write: a `done` older than that means
    somebody finished the row while this pass was working (an agent's own
    `plan_done`, or a sibling host's tick), and the report says so and names them.

    **`before` is the write, and it cannot be the report's `generated`** (PR #719
    review). `generated` is stamped before the plan is even read, and `run` reads
    OPEN rows only — so a row this pass saw as open was still open at `generated`,
    and `done < generated` is a comparison that cannot come out true in the race it
    was written to catch. It went the other way instead: every sibling's completion
    landed after `generated`, was read as ours, and the duplicated receipt the whole
    function exists to name was the one case it stayed silent about.

    The residual is clock skew between this host and the board, and it is bounded by
    the one round trip rather than by the length of the pass. Both directions cost
    one sentence in a report and neither costs a state: a fast local clock names a
    peer for a receipt that was ours, a slow one stays quiet about one that was not.

    `None` on anything unparseable, and `None` when the row was ours to record.
    Guessing either way round would put a sentence in the report that no comparison
    supports, which costs more than the line it would explain.
    """
    stamp, holder = view.get("done"), view.get("done_by")
    if not stamp or not holder:
        return None
    try:
        done, ours = datetime.fromisoformat(str(stamp)), datetime.fromisoformat(before)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return None
    if done.tzinfo is None:
        done = done.replace(tzinfo=UTC)
    if ours.tzinfo is None:
        ours = ours.replace(tzinfo=UTC)
    return str(holder) if done < ours else None


def apply_done(report: Report) -> None:
    """Complete every item whose own pull request GitHub reports merged.

    Fills :attr:`Report.applied`, :attr:`Report.apply_failed` and
    :attr:`Report.apply_declined` in place; makes no decision the report does not
    already carry.

    **A claim the board leaves is reported, not silenced.** `POST /plan/item/done`
    releases the item's claim only when it is the caller's, and this pass calls with
    a machine token and no session — so an item still held by a live agent is
    recorded done with that agent's claim left standing, and the endpoint says so in
    `claim_left`. That is the endpoint's own rule ("an item another agent is holding
    might be finished by a third, but taking their claim away is not this
    endpoint's business") and the residue this issue is about is the other case
    entirely: every one of lexray's thirteen claims had already expired. So the
    holder is carried into the report rather than dropped, because a plan row that
    went done while somebody was holding it is the one row a reader may want to ask
    about.

    **A run that could not reach GitHub writes nothing, and that falls out rather
    than being special-cased.** A ref `gh` would not resolve produces an `Unknown`
    and no finding at all, so there is nothing here to act on — the same property
    that makes an unmade check unable to read as a clean one makes it unable to read
    as a merge.
    """
    doing, declined = apply_candidates(report)
    report.apply_declined.extend(declined)
    if not doing:
        return
    try:
        client, _cfg = client_once()
    except Exception as exc:                                # noqa: BLE001
        report.apply_failed.extend(
            _attempt(f, reason=f"the board could not be reached: {exc}") for f in doing)
        return
    for finding in doing:
        note = apply_note(finding)
        # Stamped per write and not once for the batch: it is the reference
        # `_already_done` compares the board's `done` against, and a batch-wide one
        # would widen the window by however long the writes before this took.
        before = _utcnow().isoformat()
        try:
            got = client.post("/plan/item/done",
                              {"item_id": finding.item_id, "note": note})
        except Exception as exc:                            # noqa: BLE001
            # NAMED, never swallowed. A 409 is the board holding a rule this pass
            # must not route around — "a human dropped this item" is the one state
            # `done` refuses — and a rule enforced into a silence is a rule nobody
            # learns.
            report.apply_failed.append(
                _attempt(finding, reason=f"the board refused the write: {exc}"))
            continue
        view = got if isinstance(got, dict) else {}
        left = view.get("claim_left")
        report.applied.append(_attempt(
            finding, note=note,
            claim_left=(left or {}).get("holder") if isinstance(left, dict) else None,
            already=_already_done(view, before)))


# ---- reporting --------------------------------------------------------------

_HEADINGS = {
    "done_candidate": "DONE CANDIDATES — the item outlived its work",
    "dropped_candidate": "DROPPED CANDIDATES — the work was abandoned",
    "stale_claim": "STALE CLAIMS — the claim does not describe the present",
    "note_contradicted": "NOTES THE BOARD DENIES",
    "untracked_pr": "UNTRACKED — open PRs the plan does not account for",
}


def _acted(rows: list[dict], heading: str) -> list[str]:
    """One rendered block for a list the actor filled, or nothing at all."""
    if not rows:
        return []
    lines = ["", heading]
    for row in rows:
        where = f" [{short_repo(row.get('repo'))}]" if row.get("repo") else ""
        said = row.get("reason") or row.get("note") or ""
        held, first = row.get("claim_left"), row.get("already")
        lines.append(f"  {row.get('subject')}{where}: {said}"
                     + (f" — a live claim held by {held} was left" if held else "")
                     + (f" — {first} had already recorded it, so this receipt is a "
                        "second one on a row that was already done" if first else ""))
    return lines


def render(report: Report) -> str:
    """The report a cron log carries: what it did, what it found, what it could not check.

    What was APPLIED is printed above the findings and not instead of them. The
    finding is the evidence the write was made on, and a report that showed only the
    action would leave a reader unable to check the reasoning — which is the same
    argument :class:`Finding`'s own `evidence` field is annotated with, one level
    out. So a completed item appears twice, once as the disagreement and once as the
    thing done about it.
    """
    lines = [f"qb-reconcile {report.generated}",
             f"  {report.items_checked} open plan item(s), {report.prs_checked} open PR(s), "
             f"repos: {', '.join(short_repo(r) for r in report.repos) or 'none'}"]
    if report.scopes_skipped:
        # One line, once, and then nothing more about it — the whole ask of #323.
        # Above the findings rather than below, because it says what this tick's
        # scope actually WAS: the header line names the repos compared, and a
        # reader who cannot see where the plan's other scopes went is left to
        # wonder whether they were silently dropped.
        lines.append(
            f"  {len(report.scopes_skipped)} scope(s) with nothing to compare: "
            + "; ".join(f"{s['label']} ({s['items']} open item(s)) — {s['reason']}"
                        for s in report.scopes_skipped))
    if report.prs_skipped:
        # No silent filters, the same rule the fetch cap follows: "no untracked PRs"
        # and "no untracked PRs among the ones I compared" are different sentences.
        # Grouped by reason rather than one entry per PR: a repo with thirty
        # dependabot PRs open would otherwise put all thirty on one unreadable line,
        # which is the same volume problem in a different place.
        by_reason: dict[str, list[str]] = {}
        for skip in report.prs_skipped:
            by_reason.setdefault(str(skip.get("reason")), []).append(f"#{skip.get('number')}")
        lines.append(f"  {len(report.prs_skipped)} open PR(s) not compared: "
                     + "; ".join(f"{', '.join(numbers)} — {reason}"
                                 for reason, numbers in by_reason.items()))

    lines += _acted(report.applied, "COMPLETED — items whose own PR GitHub reports merged")
    lines += _acted(report.apply_failed,
                    f"NOT COMPLETED ({len(report.apply_failed)}) — the write could not be made")
    lines += _acted(report.apply_declined,
                    "LEFT ALONE — done candidates this pass does not complete")

    for condition in CONDITIONS:
        hits = [f for f in report.findings if f.condition == condition]
        if not hits:
            continue
        lines += ["", _HEADINGS[condition]]
        for f in hits:
            where = f" [{short_repo(f.repo)}]" if f.repo else ""
            lines.append(f"  {f.subject}{where}: {f.summary}")

    if report.unknowns:
        lines += ["", f"COULD NOT CHECK ({len(report.unknowns)}) — "
                      "not the same as nothing to report"]
        for u in report.unknowns:
            where = f" [{short_repo(u.repo)}]" if u.repo else ""
            lines.append(f"  {u.subject}{where} ({u.condition}): {u.reason}")

    if not report.findings:
        lines += ["", "The plan agrees with GitHub and the board on everything checked."
                      + ("" if not report.unknowns else
                         " What could not be checked is above.")]
    return "\n".join(lines)


#: Most `refs` one post carries. The board renders them as links, and a plan with
#: forty disagreements would otherwise turn one post into a ref dump — the same
#: trade `GET /reviews` makes when it publishes `changed_files_total` and not the
#: paths. Trimming loses nothing a reader needs: the post's `detail` is the whole
#: rendered report, so every finding is named there whether or not it got a link.
MAX_POST_REFS = 10


def report_findings(report: Report) -> None:
    """Hand the board this pass's findings, per scope, so `plan_read` can carry them.

    **Separate from the board post, and both are wanted.** The post is for people
    and for the stream — one line, suppressed when it would repeat itself. This is
    for `plan_read`: structured, per ref, and carrying the CONDITION, which a post
    cannot. `Ref` is a generic dev-context link with four fields, so a
    `done_candidate` and a `dropped_candidate` arrive through the post identical —
    and those two are the pair that must never be confused, one being a record
    already overtaken and the other a judgement somebody has to make (#463).

    **Sent on every pass, and NOT gated on the digest.** The write replaces a
    scope's set, so an empty report is the message that says a scope has nothing
    outstanding — skip it because "nothing changed" and rows for work that was
    finished hours ago stay on the board for ever. Idempotent by construction,
    which is what makes re-sending an unchanged report cost nothing and two hosts
    reporting the same pass harmless.

    **Per scope, because that is the unit the write replaces.** A pass covering
    two repos that posted them together would have to say which of the two an
    absent ref was absent from, and the answer would be "both" — silently
    resolving the other repo's findings.

    Best-effort, like the post beside it: a pass whose report reached the log has
    done its job, and telemetry that can fail the thing it reports on is worse
    than none.
    """
    by_repo: dict[str, list[dict]] = {repo: [] for repo in report.repos}
    # An item `--apply` completed is left out, and only that: the row exists to tell
    # a reader of the PLAN that an open item looks finished, and that item is not open
    # any more. Reported anyway, `plan_read` would hang "looks done" off a row that IS
    # done — a disagreement with nothing left to disagree with — and it would be
    # re-asserted every tick until the pass stopped finding it, which for a completed
    # item is never, because `run` reads open rows only. A write that FAILED keeps its
    # finding: there the item is still open and the disagreement still stands.
    applied = {row["item_id"] for row in report.applied if row.get("item_id")}
    for finding in report.findings:
        kind, _, value = (finding.ref or "").partition("#")
        if kind not in ("pr", "issue") or not value or not finding.repo:
            continue
        if finding.item_id and finding.item_id in applied:
            continue
        by_repo.setdefault(finding.repo, []).append({
            "ref_kind": kind, "ref_value": value,
            "condition": finding.condition,
            # The pass's own sentence, so the plan quotes what was observed rather
            # than paraphrasing it a second time on the other side.
            "said": finding.summary,
        })
    if not by_repo:
        return
    try:
        client, _cfg = client_once()
    except Exception as exc:                                # noqa: BLE001
        print(f"qb-reconcile: report stands, but the board is unreachable: {exc}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return
    for repo, findings in by_repo.items():
        try:
            client.post("/plan/reconcile", {"repo": repo, "findings": findings})
        except Exception as exc:                            # noqa: BLE001
            # NAMED, not swallowed: an older board has no such endpoint, and a
            # silent 404 every fifteen minutes is how a feature comes to be
            # believed dead. One line per scope is cheap and says which.
            print(f"qb-reconcile: {repo}'s findings were not recorded on the "
                  f"plan: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)


def post_refs(report: Report) -> list[dict]:
    """Dev-context links for the board post: the repos, then the findings' own refs.

    Repos first and unconditionally, so a post about a repo is filed under it even
    when the findings themselves are trimmed away.
    """
    refs: list[dict] = [{"kind": "repo", "value": short_repo(r)} for r in report.repos]
    for finding in report.findings[:MAX_POST_REFS]:
        if not finding.ref or not finding.repo:
            continue
        kind, _, value = finding.ref.partition("#")
        if kind in ("pr", "issue") and value:
            refs.append({"kind": kind, "value": value, "repo": finding.repo})
    return refs


def post_summary(report: Report) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """``(summary, detail)`` for the one board post ``--post`` makes."""
    # NET OF WHAT WAS COMPLETED, which is the whole reason this is not a one-liner
    # over `report.findings`. `render` lists an applied item under DONE CANDIDATES on
    # purpose — the finding is the evidence the write was made on — but the headline
    # is a count of what is OUTSTANDING, and it is the whole of this post for every
    # reader who does not open it. "9 done candidate; 4 completed" describes a plan
    # that no longer exists and reads as thirteen problems where there are five.
    settled = {row["item_id"] for row in report.applied if row.get("item_id")}
    outstanding = [f for f in report.findings
                   if not (f.item_id and f.item_id in settled)]
    counts = {c: sum(1 for f in outstanding if f.condition == c) for c in CONDITIONS}
    parts = [f"{n} {c.replace('_', ' ')}" for c, n in counts.items() if n]
    head = ", ".join(parts) if parts else "no disagreement"
    if report.applied:
        head += f"; {len(report.applied)} completed"
    if report.apply_failed:
        head += f"; {len(report.apply_failed)} could not be completed"
    if report.unknowns:
        head += f"; {len(report.unknowns)} could not be checked"
    return f"plan reconcile: {head}", render(report)


def state_path() -> str:
    """Where the digest of the last posted report lives.

    **`--post` on a 15-minute timer with no change detection is ~96 identical
    `finding` posts a day**, each carrying the whole rendered report in `detail`.
    And `finding` is not in the board's `MUTED_TYPES` (app/schemas.py), so every one
    of them lands in every agent's orient read — the volume problem that list exists
    to solve. "Post only when there is something to say" is not enough on its own,
    because unknowns are the steady state rather than the exception: a board too old
    for `cycles`, a fleet-scoped item with a ref, a hand-taken claim. So what has to
    be new is the CONTENT, and this file is how a one-shot process with no memory of
    the last tick can tell.
    """
    base = (os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME")
            or os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".local", "state"))
    return os.path.join(base, "qb-reconcile", "last-post")


def post_digest(report: Report) -> str:
    """A hash of what the report SAYS, with everything that moves on its own left out.

    Not `as_dict()`: `evidence` carries `idle_days` and GitHub's `updatedAt`, which
    change between ticks on a disagreement that has not changed at all, so hashing
    them would make every report new and buy nothing. What a reader would call "the
    same report" is the conditions, the subjects and the sentences.

    **Which puts a rule on the sentences: a `summary` or a `reason` must not carry a
    value that moves on its own.** They are hashed, so anything interpolated into one
    that changes while the disagreement does not defeats this function through the
    one field it trusts. `claim_verdict`'s live-but-quiet branch did exactly that —
    it wrote the claim's own `expires` into its reason, and `/plan` re-issues that
    timestamp on every renewal, which an agent holding an item across the claim's
    3600s TTL is *expected* to do. So the identical disagreement re-posted every
    renewal. It now says "still inside its own TTL" and leaves the timestamp to the
    board, which is where a reader can read it anyway.
    """
    body = sorted([f.condition, f.repo or "", f.ref or "", f.subject, f.summary]
                  for f in report.findings)
    body += sorted([u.condition, u.repo or "", u.ref or "", u.subject, u.reason]
                   for u in report.unknowns)
    # What the actor did belongs to what the report SAYS, and the rule above applies
    # to it unchanged: these rows carry an item id, a ref and a sentence, and no
    # timestamp. Without them a tick that completed three items hashes the same as
    # the tick before it — the findings were identical, they are what the write was
    # made on — and the one post saying the plan was repaired is the one the digest
    # suppresses.
    body += sorted(["applied", str(row.get("item_id") or ""), str(row.get("ref") or ""),
                    str(row.get("subject") or ""), str(row.get("note") or "")]
                   for row in report.applied)
    body += sorted([kind, str(row.get("item_id") or ""), str(row.get("ref") or ""),
                    str(row.get("subject") or ""), str(row.get("reason") or "")]
                   for kind, rows in (("apply_failed", report.apply_failed),
                                      ("apply_declined", report.apply_declined))
                   for row in rows)
    return hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(body, sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()


#: How long an UNCHANGED report stays suppressed before it is posted again.
#:
#: Change detection alone is "post once, then never again while the disagreement
#: persists", and that is not the same as "do not spam". `GET /board` orients over a
#: 30-minute window by default (`window_min=30`, app/api/posts.py), so a post older
#: than that is invisible to every subsequent cold orient — which is exactly the
#: reader `--post` exists for, per the timer unit's own comment that a report only
#: reaching ~/reconcile-logs is invisible to the agents whose plan it is about. A
#: disagreement that has stood for a day is not less true than a new one, and under
#: pure change detection it is the one nobody can see.
#:
#: Four hours is a compromise and worth naming as one. Matching the orient window
#: would restore continuous visibility and put back the ~96 posts a day the digest
#: was added to stop; this bounds how long a live disagreement can be invisible
#: instead of removing the possibility, at 6 posts a day for one that never changes.
REPOST_AFTER = timedelta(hours=4)


def read_digest() -> tuple[str | None, datetime | None]:
    """``(digest, when_it_was_posted)`` for the last report posted.

    ``(None, None)`` when there is nothing readable to compare against, and an
    unreadable state file is deliberately that: silencing a disagreement because a
    cache could not be read is the wrong way round. A file written before the
    timestamp existed parses as a digest with no time, which reads as "old enough"
    and posts once — the same way round.
    """
    try:
        with open(state_path(), encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            lines = fh.read().splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return None, None
    digest = (lines[0].strip() if lines else "") or None
    when = None
    if digest and len(lines) > 1:
        try:
            when = datetime.fromisoformat(lines[1].strip())
        except ValueError:
            when = None
        if when is not None and when.tzinfo is None:
            when = when.replace(tzinfo=UTC)
    return digest, when


def suppressed(digest: str, last: str | None, when: datetime | None,
               now: datetime | None = None) -> bool:
    """Should this report be held back — unchanged AND recently enough said?

    Both halves, never just the first: see :data:`REPOST_AFTER`. A last-posted time
    that could not be read is treated as long ago, because the failure that costs is
    a standing disagreement nobody can see.
    """
    if digest != last:
        return False
    if when is None:
        return False
    return (now or _utcnow()) - when < REPOST_AFTER


def write_digest(digest: str, now: datetime | None = None) -> str | None:
    """Record what was just posted, and when. Returns the reason it could not be."""
    path = state_path()
    at = (now or _utcnow()).isoformat()
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        with open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write(f"{digest}\n{at}\n")
    except OSError as exc:
        return str(exc)
    return None


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="qb-reconcile",
        description="Report where the board's plan disagrees with GitHub and /reviews.")
    parser.add_argument("--repo", metavar="OWNER/NAME",
                        help="only this repo's plan items (default: every repo the plan names)")
    parser.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", dest="as_json",
                        help="the whole report as JSON, unknowns beside the findings")
    parser.add_argument("--post", action="store_true",
                        help="also post one `finding` to the board, and only when this "
                             "report differs from the last one posted or that post has "
                             "aged past its re-post interval (off by default: this pass "
                             "reports, it does not write)")
    parser.add_argument("--apply", action="store_true",
                        help="complete every open item whose OWN ref is a pull request "
                             "GitHub reports merged, and say what it left alone and why "
                             "(off by default: this pass reports, and #552 narrowed that "
                             "rule to \"it never judges\" rather than dropping it)")
    parser.add_argument("--include-drafts", action="store_true", dest="include_drafts",
                        help="compare draft PRs against the plan too (off by default: a "
                             "draft is not yet work the plan owes an item)")
    parser.add_argument("--quiet", action="store_true",
                        help="print nothing when there is nothing to report")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    try:
        report = run(args.repo, include_drafts=args.include_drafts)
    except Unavailable as exc:
        print(f"qb-reconcile: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    # BEFORE anything is rendered, posted or gated. The report is what says what
    # this pass did, so the writes have to be in it by the time it is written out —
    # a `--json` payload or a board post that predated the actor would describe a
    # plan the same process had already changed.
    if args.apply:
        apply_done(report)

    # What counts as "something to report", for both gates below. `prs_skipped` is
    # in it: it is neither a finding nor an unknown, but it is the report's answer
    # to "what did you not compare, and why", and README and CHANGELOG both promise
    # it is never dropped silently. Keyed off findings/unknowns alone, a tick whose
    # only content was thirty skipped dependabot PRs printed nothing under `--quiet`
    # and posted nothing under `--post` — which is the shipped systemd unit's exact
    # invocation, so in the deployed configuration the feature did not exist.
    #
    # `scopes_skipped` is deliberately NOT in it (#323). A skipped PR is a filter
    # this pass applied and might have applied wrongly, so it owes an account of
    # it; a project scope having no forge behind it is a standing fact about the
    # plan, true on every tick and unchanged by anything. Counting it would make
    # `--quiet` print and `--post` post on every fifteen-minute tick forever, which
    # is the noise this issue is about, arriving through the gate rather than
    # through the report.
    #
    # `applied` and `apply_failed` are in it for the same reason, and they cannot
    # presently be non-empty without a finding beside them — a write is only ever
    # made on one. Named anyway rather than left to that coincidence: the gate should
    # say what it means, and "the pass repaired the plan" is the one line of a tick
    # under `--quiet --apply` that a reader most needs to see.
    has_content = bool(report.findings or report.unknowns or report.prs_skipped
                       or report.applied or report.apply_failed)

    if args.as_json:
        print(json.dumps(report.as_dict(), indent=2))
    elif not (args.quiet and not has_content):
        print(render(report))

    if args.post:
        # BEFORE the digest gate and outside it. The gate is about not repeating a
        # post nobody needs to read twice; the plan's copy is a state the board
        # holds, and an unchanged state still has to be re-asserted for the empty
        # case to mean anything. See report_findings.
        report_findings(report)

    if args.post and has_content:
        digest = post_digest(report)
        last, when = read_digest()
        if suppressed(digest, last, when):
            if not args.quiet:
                # stderr, because stdout under `--json` is a payload #232's orderer
                # parses and a line of prose after it would not be JSON any more.
                print("qb-reconcile: unchanged since the last post, so nothing was "
                      "posted — the report above is the current one.", file=sys.stderr)
        else:
            summary, detail = post_summary(report)
            try:
                client, _cfg = client_once()
                client.post("/post", {"type": "finding", "summary": summary,
                                      "detail": detail, "refs": post_refs(report)})
            except Exception as exc:                        # noqa: BLE001
                # Best-effort, like the panel's board recording: a pass whose report
                # reached the log has done its job, and telemetry that can fail the
                # thing it reports on is worse than none.
                print(f"qb-reconcile: report stands, but the board post failed: {exc}",
                      file=sys.stderr)
            else:
                problem = write_digest(digest)
                if problem:
                    print("qb-reconcile: posted, but the digest could not be recorded, "
                          f"so the next tick will post the same report again: {problem}",
                          file=sys.stderr)

    return report.exit_code


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
